=== Array Representation Tests ===

1. Regular arrays (1-based consecutive):
   [1, 2, 3] = [1, 2, 3]
   [10, 20, 30, 40] = [10, 20, 30, 40]
   ["a", "b", "c"] = [a, b, c]

2. 0-based arrays (associative):
   [0 => "zero", 1 => "one", 2 => "two"] = [0 => zero, 1 => one, 2 => two]
   match() result = [0 => $42.50, 1 => 42, 2 => 50]

3. Fully associative arrays (string keys):
   ["alice" => 95, "bob" => 87, "carol" => 92] = [alice => 95, bob => 87, carol => 92]
   ["x" => 10, "y" => 20, "z" => 30] = [x => 10, y => 20, z => 30]

4. Sparse arrays (associative):
   [1 => 10, 5 => 50, 10 => 100] = [1 => 10, 5 => 50, 10 => 100]
   [2 => "two", 7 => "seven"] = [2 => two, 7 => seven]

5. Empty arrays:
   [] = []

6. Nested arrays:
   [[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]] = [[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]]
   [["a" => 1, "b" => 2], ["c" => 3, "d" => 4]] = [[a => 1, b => 2], [c => 3, d => 4]]
   [match(price), match(email)] = [[0 => $42.50, 1 => 42, 2 => 50], [0 => alice@example.com, 1 => alice, 2 => example.com]]

7. Multidimensional arrays (SUBSEP keys):
   [(1,2) => "a", (1,3) => "b", (2,1) => "c"] = [12 => a, 13 => b, 21 => c]
   [("x","y") => 10, ("x","z") => 20, ("y","x") => 30] = [xy => 10, xz => 20, yx => 30]
   [(1,2,3) => "xyz", (1,2,4) => "xyz2"] = [123 => xyz, 124 => xyz2]

8. Mixed arrays (integer and string keys):
   [1 => "one", "key" => "value", 2 => "two"] = [1 => one, key => value, 2 => two]

9. Arrays with gaps (associative even if all integers):
   [1 => 10, 3 => 30, 5 => 50] = [1 => 10, 3 => 30, 5 => 50]

10. Single element arrays:
   [42] = [42]
   ["single" => "value"] = [single => value]
